<EnterpriseDetail
  slug="security"
  seoTitle="Isolation and Network Security | SAM Enterprise"
  seoDescription="How SAM isolates agent workspaces: one user per VM, an ingress firewall that only admits Cloudflare, per-node TLS keys, and short-lived tokens."
  title="The VM is the boundary"
  subtitle="Every workspace machine belongs to exactly one user. Containers inside it can be privileged so agents can run Docker, so SAM puts the hard boundary at the VM and keeps everything else scoped to it."
>
  <h2>One user per machine</h2>
  <p>
    Every node has a required owner. Placement only ever reuses the requesting user's own running or warm
    machines, so two users never share a VM, even when they work in the same shared project.
  </p>
  <p>
    One machine can host several of that user's workspaces at once. Placement packs them by the CPU and memory
    each one reserves, not by a fixed count.
  </p>

  <h2>Containers are not the boundary</h2>
  <p>
    Teams need <code>docker build</code>, Docker Compose and Testcontainers inside a workspace, so SAM applies a
    repository's devcontainer configuration as written, including privileged mode. Escaping a devcontainer
    therefore reaches the rest of that user's VM, including their other workspaces on it, but not another user's
    machine. Opening a repository in SAM trusts its devcontainer the same way GitHub Codespaces or VS Code Dev
    Containers do.
  </p>
  <p>
    A workspace can run more than one agent session (up to 10 by default). They share the workspace's files,
    so treat a workspace as one unit of trust.
  </p>

  <h2>Network</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The VM agent's port accepts connections only from Cloudflare's IP ranges. In Cloudflare Tunnel mode a machine has no public agent port at all and reaches Cloudflare through an outbound connector.</li>
    <li>Inbound SSH is dropped.</li>
    <li>Containers cannot reach the cloud provider's metadata endpoint, and machines hold no cloud-account credentials. The control plane makes every provider API call.</li>
    <li>Each machine generates its own TLS private key and gets a short-lived Cloudflare Origin CA certificate (7 days by default). No key is shared across machines.</li>
    <li><strong>Outbound traffic is not restricted.</strong> Agents can reach the internet, which they need for package installs and model APIs. Egress allowlisting is on the <a href="/enterprise/#not-yet">not-yet list</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Short-lived machines</h2>
  <p>
    When a machine's last workspace ends, it stays warm (30 minutes by default) so the same user's next task starts
    quickly, then SAM deletes it. Automatically created machines also have a 4-hour maximum lifetime. Durable
    Object alarms, a scheduled sweep and that lifetime cap all guard against forgotten machines.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two kinds of machine are long-lived on purpose: deployment nodes that host apps your agents ship, and
    machines users enroll themselves. Deployment nodes reject Compose settings that grant host
    authority, such as <code>privileged</code> and <code>network_mode</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Tokens</h2>
  <ComparisonTable
    caption="Credentials that cross the control-plane boundary, and how long they live."
    rowHeader="Token"
    columns={['Lifetime', 'Purpose']}
    rows={[
      { label: 'Session cookie', cells: ['7 days', 'Browser sign-in (HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite)'] },
      { label: 'Workspace JWT', cells: ['1 hour', 'Terminal and agent WebSocket access to one workspace'] },
      { label: 'Bootstrap token', cells: ['15 minutes, one use', 'Delivers a new machine its credentials'] },
      { label: 'Callback token', cells: ['24 hours', 'VM agent calls back to the control plane, scoped to one node or workspace'] },
    ]}
  />

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      The <a href="/docs/architecture/security/">security architecture</a> documents each of these with the code
      that enforces it, and says where a security review should focus.
    </p>
  </div>
</EnterpriseDetail>
